 You need Xcode's clang and the system SQLite. CMake and package managers are not used.
 nlohmann/json 3.12.0 is vendored in `third_party/` (MIT).
 
+On Windows (x64, Windows 10 1903 or later), with [llvm-mingw](https://github.com/mstorsjo/llvm-mingw)
+unpacked anywhere (nothing is installed):
+
+```
+python third_party\sqlite\fetch.py      SQLite's amalgamation, checked against sqlite.org's SHA3-256
+set LLVM_MINGW=C:\path\to\llvm-mingw-20261006-ucrt-x86_64
+build-windows.cmd                         build\windows-x64\: libspklib.a, spklib-host.exe, spklib-tests.exe
+build-windows.cmd test                    build and run the tests (TMP and TEMP inside build\)
+build-windows.cmd release                 build\windows-x64\release\spklib-host.exe and its .sha256
+set SPKLIB_C1_CATALOG=D:\Pictures\Test Catalog
+build\windows-x64\spklib-tests.exe
+python tests\c1_acceptance.py --host build\windows-x64\release\spklib-host.exe --catalog "<catalog>" --session "<session>" --work <new folder>
+```
+
+The helper is one static executable with SQLite compiled in. Its manifest selects the UTF-8 code page,
+so every path it takes and returns is UTF-8; it returns them with `/` between components
+(`C:/Users/…`), which every Windows file API accepts. `release` builds without a timestamp or local
+paths, so the same sources and toolchain give the same bytes.
+
