+## Capture One for Windows
+
+Diagnosed 2026-10-28/30 on a throwaway catalog and session made by **Capture One 16.6.4 for Windows**
+(`ZVERSIONINFO`: `16.6.4.3044 for Phase One Win`, compatible version 1667) with six images in two
+folders, one with a non-ASCII name. The tables and columns are the Mac's; what differs is how paths
+and parents are written:
+
+| What | Capture One for Windows | spklib (0.3.2) |
+|---|---|---|
+| The package | a plain folder `<Name>/` (no `.cocatalog` suffix) holding `<Name>.cocatalogdb`, `Adjustments/`, `Cache/`, `Originals/` and a `writelock` file while Capture One has it open | `capture_one::locate` finds the one `.cocatalogdb` in the folder either way; `writelock` is never opened |
+| Journal | `journal_mode=delete`, as on the Mac | unchanged |
+| `ZPATHLOCATION` | `ZWINROOT` `C:\`, `ZMACROOT` empty, `ZRELATIVEPATH` with `\` (`Users\<user>\Pictures\Day1`); one row per folder level, including the drive itself (`ZRELATIVEPATH` empty) | on Windows the root is `ZWINROOT` (`C:\` → `C:/`, `\` → `/`); empty `ZWINROOT` falls back to `ZMACROOT`. Elsewhere `ZMACROOT`, as before |
+| `ZPARENT` | **-1** on the root `ProjectCollection`, All Images, Trash, In Catalog and the top folder (the drive); albums at the top level point at the root (1) | -1 is "no parent": the root is never its own parent and -1 is not counted in `parents_repaired`; folders hang under the nearest folder above them, as with a missing parent row |
+| Folder rows | `CatalogFolderCollection` with `ZNAME` NULL, one per `ZPATHLOCATION` folder | named after the folder (`Day2 子文件夹`), the drive by its path (`C:/`) |
+| Album groups | a group ("Trips") is a `VirtualFolderCollection` under the root; an album in it points at it | `group`, the album under it |
+| Ratings, colour tags | `ZVARIANTMETADATA` on the combined layer, as on the Mac (red 1, yellow 3, green 4, blue 5, pink 6) | unchanged |
+
+Windows' file locks are mandatory: while Capture One holds SQLite's write locks, reading the bytes
+SQLite locks (at 1 GiB, in a page SQLite never uses for data) fails. Only a database larger than 1 GiB
+has that page. The snapshot copy opens the database with `CreateFileW` sharing read, write and delete,
+and writes zeros for that page (its size from the header) instead of reading it; the copy is then
+opened through a `file:///C:/…?mode=ro&immutable=1` URI.
+
 ## How it is read without risk
 
 1. `capture_one::locate` finds `<Name>.cocatalogdb` in the package.
